        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-25238",
        ":question/problem": "If the expression ${x}^{2}+4x+5$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2{)}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2$ and $q=1.$"
      },
